d conversations very good exchange of  views we're keeping very close touch  they understand how a monetary policy  works and i think in the report  uh i think there is an acknowledgement  that  in a small open economy the exchange  rate matters more than interest rate and  therefore we've chosen to  target the exchange rate and that means  sometimes you have to you have to  intervene in both directions  um to keep the exchange rate on it's on  on the  prescribed path so i think they  understand that um  but they have a framework which they  need to apply globally  uh they want to make sure that currency  intervention is not carried out with the  purpose of gaining unfair  export competitiveness advantage for  kernel corn purposes we've we've  explained to them extensively that it's  never the way  that's never the way we operate our  exchange rate is solely guided by  inflation and growth considerations the  typical monetary policy considerations  not the current account on the trade  account we've also announced that we  will be transparent  in our exchange rate interventions we  will beginning next year next july  uh announce how much we have intervened  but we can't do it in real time for  obvious reasons  obvious tactical reasons uh but we are  not we don't have a problem uh  disclosing how much we intervened in the  preceding six months  so that people know how much we've  intervened is that concern given the  uncertainty  or the unpredictability of the trauma  administration is that concern that  could be used  in the event of a trade war for instance  uh  well we actually run a trade deficit  with the united states  so there ought not to be a complaint on  that front  so i don't see this becoming an issue  and like i said we've  although we've been named in the report  uh we've had  very good conversations with the u.s  treasury ongoing dialogues  exchange of views very constructive and  fruitful we know where they're coming  from we understand what their co