world that because  of what they did fabulously during the  global financial crisis  and subsequently in holding up uh  the global economy and global financial  system there's now tremendous  unrealistic expectations placed on  central banks that  every rough patch that we run into uh  monetary policy is going to get us out  of it  i think that is wrong and i think it's  also wrong for central banks to feed  that  expectation monetary policy provides the  basis for sustained non-inflationary  economic growth  it provides stability predictability  over the medium term that the value of  money  will be will be stable and that is the  basis for then carrying on economic  activities  but it can't be that every slowdown  every uh risk or threat on the horizon  has to be addressed by a loosening of  monetary policy  um i think fiscal policy has a strong  role to play  and that has not been sufficiently  addressed  partly because too much of the weight  has been placed in monetary policy so i  think we need a better balance between  the two  otherwise it is exceedingly unfair on  the central banking community  speaking of the global economy let's  talk about possibly a looming global  recession singapore escape a technical  recession  and uh the mes eased  monetary policy but not to the extent  that some people expected i mean what's  driving some of the optimism that you  see  perhaps in 2020 well i think uh  you always want to keep some powder dry  and not deplete all your policy buffers  uh singapore is fortunate we've got both  monetary and fiscal policy buffers  quite quite good uh we our public  finances are strong  and we can if we want uh uh if it's felt  necessary  apply more fiscal stimulus likewise  we've been on an appreciating path  of the exchange rate for some time now  so that's sufficient buffer there  we have reduced the slope so the rate of  appreciation is lower  let's see how the data comes out over  the next two quarters  a key assessment to be made is whether  this is bottoming out and whether we're  going to see a modest recovery  next year if that's the case then i  think we're in  pretty good shape but if things were to  take a turn for the worse  then i think you need some buffer you  need some ammunition and so we're  keeping some powder dry  and we've said as much that if necessary  we prepared to use it  and there's still space  speaking of space i mean what policy  tools have not been used because when  you take a look at central banks  and what they're saying right now  they're willing to cut rates they're  willing to go to zero they're going  willing to go to negative they're  talking about qe for singapore  how might qe look like if  you need a qe of some form  singapore wouldn't need qe because our  monetary policy is based on the exchange  rate  managing the exchange rate so we're not  subject to the  zero lower bound of interest rates and  even there they think of negative  interest rates and qe as supplements  with an exchange rate centered monetary  policy those considerations don't apply  um we can if necessary  bring the exchange rate path to to to  zero as we've done before  uh we've never before uh uh had a policy  of deliberately depreciating the  currency  um but we can't re-center the exchange  rate downwards which we've done before  too  if we think the current rate of the  exchange rate is too tight uh we can  always  re-center the band downwards and  with that flexibility there is actually  no constraint  so we have fair amount of policy space  on on the monetary front  without having to think about qe ravi  back in may  the u.s treasury put singapore for the  first time  in the watch list for monetary practices  currency practices and by rule  in its upcoming report it will include  singapore what's your take  on whether there's been a  misunderstanding of how  singapore carries out its currency  practices  yes we've been in very close touch with  the u.s treasury on this uh very  good conversations very good exchange of  views we're keeping very close touch  they understand how a monetary policy  works and i think in the report  uh i think there is an acknowledgement  that  in a small open economy the exchange  rate matters more than interest rate and  therefore we've chosen to  target the exchange rate and that means  sometimes you have to you have to  intervene in both directions  um to keep the exchange rate on it's on  on the  prescribed path so i think they  understand that um  but they have a framework which they  need to apply globally  uh they want to make sure that currency  intervention is not carried out with the  purpose of gaining unfair  export competitiveness advantage for  kernel corn purposes we've we've  explained to them extensively that it's  never the way  that's never the way we operate our  exchange rate is solely guided by  inflation and growth considerations the  typical monetary policy considerations  not the current account on the trade  account we've also announced that we  will be transparent  in our exchange rate interventions we  will beginning next year next july  uh announce how much we have intervened  but we can't do it in real time for  obvious reasons  obvious tactical reasons uh but we are  not we don't have a problem uh  disclosing how much we intervened in the  preceding six months  so that people know how much we've  intervened is that concern given the  uncertainty  or the unpredictability of the trauma  administration is that concern that  could be used  in the event of a trade war for instance  uh  well we actually run a trade deficit  with the united states  so there ought not to be a complaint on  that front  so i don't see this becoming an issue  and like i said we've  although we've been named in the report  uh we've had  very good conversations with the u.s  treasury ongoing dialogues  exchange of views very constructive and  fruitful we know where they're coming  from we understand what their concerns  are  they understand where we're operating  from so i think  i'm pretty hopeful that uh that you know  being named in the report is one thing  but whether it leads to  to adverse consequences is quite another  which i don't think is likely  just one final question if you could  oblige me what keeps you up at night  when you ask this question to to bankers  um you get a very similar answer  i think it's about technology risk  because all the stuff we've talked about  economic slowdown trade wars they're not  entirely new you can figure out  what the likely consequences are what  the likely counter measures might be  and so on traditional financial  stability issues i think we've got a  reasonably good handle on it  since the last crisis i think the  financial stability board has done a  very good job  i think we have not paid enough  attention to technology related risks  and the pervasive use of technology has  been hugely beneficial  but i'm not sure all the players  involved this means both financial  institutions  consumers central banks regulators and  governments  whether all of us have a really good  handle on  the various kinds of cyber risks um  that or more broadly technology-related  risks  that can potentially have systemic  consequences  and can bring down institutions  potentially or bring down  or create gridlock in the financial  system ravi menon thank you so much for  your insight today been a pleasure sir  thank you thank you linda thank you
 

